# Local additional context (agent-isolation container)

You are running inside an `agent-isolation` Docker container — an isolated
Claude Code environment. The host machine is not directly reachable from
inside this container; everything you do happens here unless explicitly
routed out.

This file is the *container's* `local-additional-context.md`. The host has
its own copy at the same path with different content (the user's host
stance). They never see each other — same path, location-specific content.

## Where things live

| Container path        | Mount mode | What it is                                            |
|-----------------------|------------|-------------------------------------------------------|
| `/workspace/<name>`   | `rw`       | Project source the user is working on. Edit here.    |
| `/reference/<name>`   | `ro`       | Read-only references. Don't write.                    |
| `/mcp/<name>`         | `ro`       | Local MCP-server sources. Read-only.                  |
| `/home/agent/.claude` | `claude`   | Your synced claude config (skills, plugins, settings).|

`~/.claude.json` is symlinked into `/home/agent/.claude/.claude.json` by
the entrypoint, so user-profile state persists across container restarts.

## Reaching service containers

When `launch.sh` brings up service containers (MongoDB,
Elasticsearch, etc.), they live on a user-defined docker network and are
reachable **by their compose service name, not by `localhost`**:

- `http://elasticsearch:9200`
- `mongodb://mongodb:27017`
- `http://<service-name>:<port>` generally

`localhost` inside this container is the container itself.

## Port forwarding

Container ports are **fixed at `docker run` time**. If a project needs a
port the container wasn't started with, the user has to
`docker rm <agent-name>` and relaunch with `--port HOST:CTNR`. There is
no way to add a port to a running container.

The Slack OAuth callback port (3118) is always published.

## Networking out

- Outbound internet works (npm/pnpm install, git fetch, curl, etc.).
- The host machine is reachable as `host.docker.internal` (Docker
  Desktop) — use this when you need to talk to a service running on
  the host, e.g. claude-monitor's central daemon.
- Containers on the same user-defined network reach each other by name
  (see above).

## Baked-in tools

The agent image (`claude-agent:latest`) ships with:

- Node.js 20, `npm`, `pnpm`
- Claude Code CLI, `claude-powerline` (statusline)
- `git`, `curl`, `jq`, `vim-tiny`, `rsync`, `zsh`, `procps`
- System Chromium at `/usr/bin/chromium` (`CHROME_PATH` env var points
  here; `agent-browser` and other `CHROME_PATH`-aware tools use it
  automatically)
- `agent-browser` (preferred CLI for headless-browser work in this
  environment, in place of chrome-devtools MCP)

**Playwright note:** `PLAYWRIGHT_SKIP_BROWSER_DOWNLOAD=1` is set, so
`pnpm install`/`npm install` won't fetch Playwright's browsers. Two ways
to get a working browser:
- Run `npx playwright install chromium` after install (Playwright's own
  pinned browser, ~200MB).
- OR pass `executablePath: "/usr/bin/chromium"` to `chromium.launch()`
  to use the system Chromium directly (Playwright does NOT auto-read
  `CHROME_PATH`).

If you need a tool not listed, install with `npm install -g`,
`pnpm add -g`, or `apt-get install` (root-equivalent inside the container
isn't available; user-local installs work).

## Multi-shell workflow

A second `launch.sh --config <same>.agent.yml` invocation while the
container is running attaches via `docker exec`. The useful pattern:

- **Terminal 1:** launch container; in the container shell, start
  long-lived servers detached:

  ```
  nohup pnpm dev > /tmp/web.log 2>&1 & disown
  ```

- **Terminal 2:** second `launch.sh` → enters the same container;
  run `claude --dangerously-skip-permissions` here.

- `claude /exit` in terminal 2 doesn't kill the servers in terminal 1;
  they keep running.

**When you start a long-running process from inside the agent (claude)
session, detach it.** `nohup … & disown`, redirect output to a file in
`/tmp/`, not the terminal. Output bleeding into the TUI makes the
session unusable.

## claude-monitor (if active, as a worked `on_start` example)

If the user is monitoring this session via claude-monitor:

- The `claude-monitor` plugin is enabled in `~/.claude/settings.json`.
- Its hook (`forward-event.sh`) POSTs events to `MONITOR_URL` (default
  `http://127.0.0.1:4820`).
- The local-daemon (which listens on `4820` and forwards to the host's
  central daemon at `host.docker.internal:4830`) runs **automatically
  if you set `on_start` in the agent config**:

  ```yaml
  on_start:
    command: node /reference/stt-private/tools/claude-monitor/packages/local-daemon/src/index.js
    env:
      CENTRAL_URL: http://host.docker.internal:4830
    log: /tmp/local-daemon.log
  ```

  agent-isolation runs the command backgrounded at every container
  start (fresh create AND resume). Tail the log at the path above to
  confirm. `on_start` is generic — claude-monitor is one user of the
  affordance; you can run any command this way.

- If you prefer to start it manually, the equivalent recipe is:

  ```
  CENTRAL_URL=http://host.docker.internal:4830 \
    nohup node /reference/stt-private/tools/claude-monitor/packages/local-daemon/src/index.js \
    > /tmp/local-daemon.log 2>&1 & disown
  ```

## Don't assume

- `localhost` does not reach the host.
- Tools not listed above may not be present — check first.
- Port mappings can't be configured at runtime; they're docker-run-time.
- `/reference/<name>` is read-only — don't write there.
